<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry David Thoreau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1862">1862</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as David Henry Thoreau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry David Thoreau was an American essayist, poet and philosopher, born in Concord, Massachusetts, and a leading transcendentalist. From July 1845 he spent two years in a small house he had built on land owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a> by Walden Pond, the experiment in simple living he recounted in Walden (1854). His essay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">Civil Disobedience</a>, first published as Resistance to Civil Government, argued for disobedience to an unjust state and later influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> A lifelong abolitionist, he attacked the fugitive slave law and defended John Brown, and his writings on natural history anticipated ecology. He died of tuberculosis in 1862.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-david-thoreau.jpg" alt="Henry David Thoreau" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin D. Maxham active 1848 - 1858 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_D._Maxham_-_Henry_David_Thoreau_-_Restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry David Thoreau</b>’s Walden (1854) argued that people should become intimately close with nature, and John Muir came to believe in nature’s inherent right.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Individualist anarchism, drawing on Godwin, Stirner and <b>Henry David Thoreau</b>, puts the individual first; social anarchism seeks freedom through equality and social ownership.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation. What is called resignation is confirmed desperation.&#8221;</p><footer>Walden (1854), &quot;Economy&quot; &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Walden_(1854)_Thoreau/Economy" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately, to front only the essential facts of life, and see if I could not learn what it had to teach, and not, when I came to die, discover that I had not lived.&#8221;</p><footer>Walden (1854), &quot;Where I Lived, and What I Lived For&quot; &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Walden_(1854)_Thoreau/Where_I_Lived,_and_What_I_Lived_for" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If a man does not keep pace with his companions, perhaps it is because he hears a different drummer. Let him step to the music which he hears, however measured or far away.&#8221;</p><footer>Walden (1854), &quot;Conclusion&quot; &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Walden_(1854)_Thoreau/Conclusion" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Under a government which imprisons any unjustly, the true place for a just man is also a prison.&#8221;</p><footer>Resistance to Civil Government (1849) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Resistance_to_Civil_Government" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
